Author: Arne Juul <arnej@vespa.ai>
Date:   Mon Aug 31 20:00:09 2026 +0000

    Allocate Einsum's CUDA intermediates on the stream that uses them
    
    Concurrent Run() calls on one CUDA session can corrupt each other's device
    memory. EinsumOp::DeviceHelpers::CudaDeviceHelpers::CreateTensor builds every
    intermediate with Tensor::Create(type, shape, allocator), which reaches the
    Tensor constructor and calls allocator->Alloc(len) with no stream.
    
    A stream-aware arena only tags a chunk when it is told which stream the buffer
    belongs to, and FindChunkPtr treats an untagged chunk as safe for any stream to
    take. Einsum's intermediates are written by queued work - DataCopy issues
    cudaMemcpyAsync and ZeroBuffer cudaMemsetAsync on the run's stream - but the
    unique_ptr releases them as soon as it leaves scope inside Compute(), while
    that work is still in flight. Another request's thread then picks the same
    memory up on a different stream, with nothing synchronizing the two. The
    resulting garbage reaches a kernel as an out-of-range address and surfaces as a
    sticky cudaErrorIllegalAddress on whatever API call comes next.
    
    Instrumenting the arena on a 12-layer BERT encoder under concurrent load, every
    untagged device allocation during a run came from these two call sites (the
    Transpose and MatMul wrappers), and 49 of the resulting chunks changed hands
    between threads with a minimum gap of 16 us after the free.
    
    Add a Tensor constructor that takes the stream and allocates through
    AllocOnStream when the allocator supports it, and give the CreateTensor device
    helper the einsum_cuda_assets parameter its siblings already carry so the CUDA
    implementation can pass the run's stream. Diagonal, which allocates its output
    directly, gets the same treatment. The CPU implementation ignores the argument.
    
    Einsum's reduce path does not go through CreateTensor at all: the wrapper hands
    straight to the device ReduceSum helper, which allocates its output inside
    cuda::ReductionOps::ReduceCompute. That function's only caller in the tree is
    Einsum and it already takes the stream as a parameter, so it is fixed here too
    rather than left for the sweep.
    
    Other CUDA EP kernels build per-run intermediates the same way (softmax,
    expand, identity/sequence copies); those are left for a follow-up.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

